{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nglnp3drepvpw43kamqbqox5zbx54amtp7u3j2foiwhlv2wuwzja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **No divergence was found in the Rust translation** — it matches the C reference byte-for-byte everywhere.\n\n## Gate status\n\n| gate | result |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` — `nm -D` parity | C **615** / Rust **615**, **0 missing, 0 extra**, 0 non-libc undefined |\n| Phase B — `CONFIGS.md` | **171/171 rows** pass across randomized inputs |\n| Phase C — `ERRORS.md` | **1269/1269 rejection sites** have a passing error-path test comparing the exact error code |\n| Phase D — feature combos | `Cargo.toml` declares no `[features]`, `grep 'cfg(feature'` = 0 hits → **1 configuration**, and it passes |\n\n**287 tests across 19 files**, all through `libloading`/`dlsym` on both `.so`s (no direct Rust calls; the crate is `cdylib`-only, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrappers are what runs). The two objects are provably distinct — the C `.so` has `SONAME libzstd.so`, the Rust cdylib has none, and symbol addresses are asserted to differ.\n\n## What the divergence hunt actually turned up\n\nEvery apparent mismatch resolved to the C, not the Rust — recorded with file:line evidence in `CONFIGS.md`:\n\n- **13 upstream C crashes / OOB accesses** both libraries reproduce identically. Notably: `ZSTD_compressSequences` writes **70 bytes below `dst`** on a short destination; the super-block writer writes **past `dst + dstCapacity`** when `ZSTD_c_targetCBlockSize` is set; `stableIn_notConsumed` survives `ZSTD_CCtx_reset` and underflows into the caller's buffer on the next session. Where possible these rows stay differential via 64 KiB canary guard bands so the whole padded region is still compared.\n- **13 documented C preconditions** whose violation is UB (e.g. `ZSTD_selectBlockCompressor` indexes its dispatch tables unvalidated; `XXH32_reset(NULL)` memsets NULL because `assert` is compiled out).\n- **2 unspecified-value cases.** The interesting one: `ZSTD_execSequence` does an unconditional 16-byte `ZSTD_copy16` even for a 2-byte literal run, leaking up to 14 bytes of never-written `litBuffer` into `dst` before returning `corruption_detected`. I proved the Rust faithful by re-running through `ZSTD_initStaticDCtx` over a workspace pre-filled with 0x11/0x22/0x00 — C and Rust then emit byte-identical output — so `phase_c_decompress` runs all context-based decodes on static workspaces and keeps comparing error-path output.\n\n## Suite sensitivity (measured, not assumed)\n\nMutating `ZSTD_hash4` to a constant fails **38+ tests across 5 files**. Two mutations correctly fail *nothing*: `^ 1` (shifted out by `>> (32 - hashLog)`) and `^ 0x80000000` — XOR on a hash output is a bijection on the index space, so it permutes table slots without changing collisions. That experiment exposed a real gap: rows 32/33 drew one random `(size, class)` per `(strategy, minMatch, windowLog)` triple and could draw a 0-byte input, skipping the match finder. Both rows now iterate the size ladder and content classes explicitly.\n\nArtifacts are regenerable — `translation/tools/` holds the extractors, `run_all_features.sh` derives feature combos from `Cargo.toml` mechanically, and `VERIFICATION.md` has the full inventory and repro steps."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":223,"cache_read_input_tokens":617315,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":223,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b0ef8e1e-2e1d-45e6-be23-c299770ecb9a","uuid":"8fc6444d-22ed-48ad-893e-0c6230ec0792","timestamp":"2026-09-04T06:31:41.243Z"}
